%I A398673 #5 Aug 11 2026 13:44:16
%S A398673 0,1,1,2,7,43,331,6585,1926642,1517235958
%N A398673 Number of disconnected quasi-regular graphs on 2*n + 1 vertices.
%C A398673 A quasi-regular graph is a simple graph in which one vertex has degree d+1 and every other vertex has degree d, for some d. By the handshaking lemma, its order and d are both odd.
%C A398673 For fixed d, let A_d(N), C_d(N), and R_d(N) count all quasi-d graphs, connected quasi-d graphs, and all d-regular graphs on N vertices, respectively, with R_d(0) = 1. Unique component decomposition gives A_d(N) = Sum_j C_d(j)*R_d(N-j), and hence A_d(N)-C_d(N) = Sum_{j<N} C_d(j)*R_d(N-j).
%C A398673 The order-15 term decomposes as 331 = 1 + 307 + 23, contributed by base degrees d = 1, 3, and 5, respectively. There are no disconnected contributions for d >= 7 at order 15: a disconnected graph would require a quasi-d component on at least d+2 vertices and a nonempty d-regular component on at least d+1 vertices, totaling at least 2*d+3 > 15.
%H A398673 Eric W. Weisstein, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Quasi-RegularGraph.html">Quasi-Regular Graph</a>, MathWorld.
%F A398673 a(n) = A398642(n) - A398643(n). For fixed d, A_d(N)-C_d(N) = Sum_{j<N} C_d(j)*R_d(N-j), where R_d(0) = 1.
%e A398673 a(10) = 1517235958 because 1517235958 quasi-regular graphs on 21 vertices are disconnected.
%Y A398673 Cf. A398642 (all quasi-regular graphs), A398643 (connected quasi-regular graphs), A005638 (cubic graphs), A165626 (quintic graphs), and A165628 (septic graphs).
%K A398673 nonn,more
%O A398673 1,4
